nto rivers is not needed to “come back” — it’s replaced by entirely new mass. If you stand at a glacier’s terminus in spring, you see bare ice. If you return in autumn after the melt season, you might see a bit of new snow high up but still a lot of bare ice. Then winter arrives and buries everything under a thick blanket of snowfall. A local might say, “It freezes back in winter,” because the landscape transforms from wet, dripping ice to a solid, snow-covered frozen world. They’re describing the visible outcome (the glacier is clean and white again, the surface is frozen solid), not the microphysics. That’s completely intelligible and not false — it just doesn’t mean liquid water ran back uphill and froze. ### What about the meltwater refreezing that *does* happen? As I explained before, some meltwater *does* refreeze within the glacier’s upper layers during the summer. This internal refreezing is vital for mass retention, and it can be the difference between a glacier slowly shrinking or holding steady. However, the volume of this refrozen meltwater is modest compared to the annual snowfall. For a typical temperate glacier, refrozen melt might account for 5–15% of the total winter mass gain; the remaining 85–95% is new snow. In very cold, dry polar glaciers, the share can be higher because so little snow falls — refreezing of melt within the firn becomes a larger fraction of the mass budget. That’s likely why you’ve read articles emphasizing this cycle. It’s real, but it’s not the primary mechanism that prevents glaciers from disappearing overnight. ### So why are glaciers still there (for now)? Because for centuries, the huge input of winter snowfall has balanced or exceeded summer melt. The moment that balance tips — and it has for the vast majority of the world’s glaciers — they begin to shrink. They haven’t vanished *yet* because they still have stored ice from millennia of past snowfall, and in many places, some winter accumulation still occurs. But they are